    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having some trouble with a few recent dermatology claims. We're seeing a lot of atypical presentations that don't seem to fit the standard L-series codes perfectly. Specifically, when a patient presents with irregular, nodular growths that have a somewhat greenish or mottled discoloration, but it doesn't clearly meet the criteria for a specific malignancy or a common benign lesion, what's the best way to approach the hierarchy? Should we be looking at more specific morphology codes or just defaulting to \"other specified\" under the L-category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In such cases, a more nuanced approach may be necessary. You could consider using higher-level codes like L51 (Melanocytic nevi, large or atypical) to encompass the irregular, nodular growths with possible mottled discoloration, as it provides more specificity than the standard L-series codes. Additionally, seeking guidance from your institution's coding guidelines or dermatology experts can help clarify the best course of action. A detailed clinical assessment and review of images may also aid in accurately classifying the lesions.",
